  "summary": "Distinctive temple architecture from Kerala featuring timber roofs, sloping roofs, and courtyard layouts.",
  "description": "Characterized by timber structures with sloping roofs, inner courtyards (nalukettu), and distinctive roof shapes (thitti). Developed from indigenous building traditions and Buddhist influences, reflecting Kerala's unique cultural and environmental adaptations.",
  "culturalContext": "Kerala's timber-roof temples — Vadakkumnathan, Tali, Koodalmanikyam — are the Dravidian South's exception: not stone gopurams but sloped copper-and-timber roofs (two-tiered sreekovil sanctums, namaskara mandapa porches) against monsoon, with laterite plinths and murals inside. Craft rules (thachu shastra) govern roof pitches and joinery; the temples' circular (vatta) and square (chathura) sanctum plans, ringed by outer walls and kshetrapala shrines, make Kerala's sacred skyline uniquely tiered and steep.",
